---
title: Healthcare Access for All
order: 1
icon: heart
---

Nairobi County's healthcare system is in crisis. Our public hospitals — Kenyatta National Referral, Mbagathi, Pumwani Maternity, and the network of level-2 and level-3 facilities — are chronically overcrowded, understaffed, and plagued by recurring drug stock-outs. Patients wait hours, sometimes days, for emergency care. Mothers deliver babies on corridor floors. This is not the Nairobi we deserve.

We demand a fully equipped health center in every ward, staffed by at least one medical officer, three nurses, a clinical officer, and a pharmacist — operating 24 hours a day, seven days a week. No ward should be more than five kilometers from a functioning public health facility. We demand a county-wide electronic health supply chain system to end drug stock-outs once and for all, with real-time inventory tracking and automated reordering. Every health center must have reliable electricity, clean water, and functional diagnostic equipment.

Maternal and child health must be the cornerstone of our county health agenda. We demand expanded mother-and-child clinics in every sub-county, free maternity care with dignity, and a network of community health promoters who visit every household in their assigned zone. No mother should die giving life. No child should succumb to a preventable disease because a vaccine was unavailable or a clinic was too far.

The bill for this transformative agenda is modest compared to the cost of inaction. By ring-fencing 30 percent of the county budget for health, eliminating ghost workers from the payroll, and entering into a framework agreement with the Kenya Medical Supplies Authority (KEMSA) for transparent, timely procurement, we can deliver the healthcare every Nairobian deserves. This is not a campaign promise — it is a moral obligation.
